tance speech by the film’s creative leadership (including producers).\n\n---\n\n### 3) **Visual nationality indicators (flags, text, spoken words)?**\n\n✅ **Strong indicators.**  \n- The individuals on stage (Bong Joon-ho, Han Jin-won, Kwak Sin-ae, etc.) are ethnically Korean and are widely known as South Korean filmmakers.\n- Although no South Korean flag is visible in these frames, the global context of the 2020 Oscars (when *Parasite* won Best Picture) is well-documented. The film is a South Korean production.\n- In frame 27, the group is seen celebrating — their attire, appearance, and the context of the win (first non-English language film to win Best Picture) strongly associate them with South Korea.\n- The acceptance speech (though audio is not available here) is famously known to have included Korean phrases and references to South Korea.\n\n---\n\n### 4) **People holding Oscar statuettes?**\n\n✅ **Yes.**  \nFrames 6, 11, 20, and 27 clearly show Bong Joon-ho and others holding the Oscar statuette, confirming they are the recipients of the award.\n\n---\n\n### 5) **Presenters announcing Best Picture?**\n\n🟡 **Not visible in these frames.**  \nThe frames do not show the presenters announcing the winner — they focus on the reaction and acceptance by the winners. However, the “BEST PICTURE” text on stage (frames 24, 28, 29) confirms the category being awarded.\n\n---\n\n### 6) **Acceptance speech content.**\n\n🟡 **Not directly verifiable from visuals alone.**  \nHowever, in frames 12–15 and 17–19, Bong Joon-ho is speaking at the podium — consistent with an acceptance speech. While we cannot hear the words, the global context (and widely available footage) confirms he spoke in Korean and English, thanking the Academy and referencing South Korea.\n\n---\n\n### Final Determination:\n\n✅ **Answer '2' (Koreans) is correct.**  \nThe producers of the Best Picture-winning film *Parasite* are South Korean. The frames confirm:\n- This is the Best Picture award.\n- The rec